<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gidan Rumfa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 15th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Emir's Palace, Kano</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gidan Rumfa is the palace of the emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, built by Sarki <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-rumfa">Muhammad Rumfa</a> in the late 15th century. Set in a large walled compound, it has remained the royal residence ever since, used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> of the early 19th century, and is one of the oldest continuously occupied palaces in Nigeria.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gidan-rumfa.jpg" alt="Gidan Rumfa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jonathan Riddell; Flickr User: jriddell; https://www.flickr.com/photos/jriddell/ · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gate_to_the_Gidan_Rumfa_(2009)_in_Kano,_Nigeria..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
